Short:    Trion Shareware BBS update V1.10 -> V1.12
Uploader: david.ellison3@ukonline.co.uk (Dave Ellison)
Author:   paul.spijkerman@ddimedia.com (Paul Spijkerman)
Type:     Comm/BBS


Trion BBS is a ShareWare multinode BBS with RIPscrip BBS graphics,
echomail, mailer with EMSI & IEMSI , areafix, tick processor,
allfiles, SplitChatDoor, CDRomDoor etc etc..
Controllable through scripts (by a Cron utility).
Fax and Voice programmes are spawnable.
Runs CliDoors, ParagonDoors, Arexx Doors, Max's BBS Doors.



    Major changes from version V1.10 to V1.12   (Shortlist)



     Utils:   BBS, TrionRM, Poll, Areafix, TMP, MailDecode, AttViewer
     Doors:   
     Scripts: 
     Cfg:     



   -Changed the mail processor (And BBS, Poll and areafix) so
    you can put mail on hold. Which means that mail only is send
    when some one calls you. It is not send when you call the
    node. This works also with attached files but not with .tic
    files at this moment. 

 !! You need to change the systems.cfg
    All lines have to be changed from:

    <system>  <archiver>  <Password>

    into:

    <system>  <archiver>  <send>  <Password>

    Where send can be either SEND or HOLD


   -Changed Areafix so you can connect and disconnect multiple sections
    with a wildcard.

    Because my wildcard function is case insensitive it doesn't
    matter anymore if you type the area name in upper or lower
    case. So "*amiga*" or "*AMIGA*" will both connect or
    disconnect all sections with "amiga" in the TagName.

   -Added options to areafix so you can 'pause' or 'resume' a node
    from the command line.

   -Finally got my Tape-streamer online again and got the old
    Areafix source from tape for the connect/disconnect functions
    without Wildcards.
    Build those in areafix for "Resume" which doesn't need
    wildcards. It's more suitable and much faster.

   -Checked the integrity of all the BBS sources.
    If all sources are complete and if there are source
    files which are not needed but only where placed there
    as an example.

   -Changed the BBS a little so when you reply to a message you
    first get some information about the original message at the
    top of the screen.

   -If you start the BBS with the -B flag, it will not check if
    a user has enough time to download a file when it is marked.
    This is usefull if you have a crazy modem which doesn't give
    a connection speed, so the calculated transfer time is hours
    instead of minutes :)

   -Fixed a little bug in the RipScrip engine, it used the wrong
    function to draw the last line of a filled polygon.
    Which could sometimes give the wrong result.

   -Changed MailDecode so if there is a name provided with a UUencoded
    file an attempt is made to use it.
    If there is a problem (a dupe for instance) the old name convention
    is used.

    Looked if I could do the same with "MIME" encoded files,
    but I don't have enough information.
    The names and storage methodes changes places in the headers.
    With the MIME methode files can be encoded with BASE64 or text
    files can added unencoded.
    (Base64 coded files are a little smaller then UUencoded ones).

    Base64 files are still recognized the old way, by checking
    if a line is Base64 compliant.

   -Changed the mailer so that if after a "send session" a flo file is
    remade that and file is "in use" because it was processed by
    the mail processor it is added again instead of orphaned.

   -Changed the mail processor so that archive names are only added
    to a .FLO file when it is completely ready. No more packets
    to add.

   -Changed the mail processor so empty "PATH:" lines are
    recognized (they where only recognized if PATH: was
    followed with a space).
    Otherwise you may get "no path" message while you
    read messages, if the message was written by a point
    directly "under" your node.

   -Changed TrionRM so when you are online and you get a
    "you have a message waiting" text, the name of the writer
    is included.

   -Added the time a line was received to the AttentionViewer
    for people who have it always open.

    You have to run AttViewer with the flag -T to get the time
    on the screen.

    Also added the possibility to define the width of the window,
    just put the number of columns after the word attviewer.
    The default is still 40, but if you use the -T flag, 50
    maybe better.

   -Added a tildecode ~107 (#107) which does the same as code 106
    (displaying the type of message section) but with only
    3 characters.


   -Added a simple "mailing list" function to the BBS.
    When you give a username when writing a message, the
    BBS will check if there is a file with that name in
    the directory "Trion:".
    If there is such a file, the message is send to all
    people in the file.

    The file has the following format:
    If the section has an E-mail flag:

    "recipient name"     "E-mail address"

    If the section has no E-mail flag (just netmail):

    "recipient name"     "fidostyle nodenumber"

    Note that if there are spaces in a name or address it has
    to be places between "", because only 2 arguments on a
    line are accepted.


   -Changed the BBS a little bit so it sends the CPS of the
    last send file to a door, if a file was downloaded from
    a door. Here follows a little modified TestDownload()
    function from the DemoDoor suplied in the main archive:




void TestDownload(struct DoorIO *pd, struct ADS_Data *pp)
{
   UWORD cps;
   int   result;

   nprintf(pd, "Let's mark the startup-sequence\r\n");

   /* mark as much files as needed before calling download */

/* MarkFile(pp , "s:startup-sequence" ); */
   MarkFile(pp , "dh2:hal9002.lha" );

   Delay(100);

   nprintf(pd, "Here it comes\r\n");
   result = Download( pp );

   /* 0=OK, 1=Err, -1,-2=trouble with msgs */

   nprintf(pd, "\nresult = %d\n",  result);

   if (result == 0){
      cps = pp->BBSMsg->ADS_Data_word;
      nprintf(pd, "\nCPS = %d\n",  cps);
   }else{
      nprintf(pd, "\nError\n");
   }

   Delay(100);
}



